= Feminism beyond suffrage?

Our party has been a vehement defender of the constitutional rights of women to be treated the same as men. By this point, we have made great progress both in addressing wage disparity and the restrictions marriage pushes upon both employed and unemployed women[? if abortion_rights: , and even pushed through a bill to legalize abortion despite the conservative opposition ?]. However, all our efforts show that it is not enough: our enemy ought to be the patriarchal culture so prevalent in Germany: without tackling this foundation, no Constitution can overcome the misogynistic sentiments entrenched this deep.

We would need to take into account the needs of both "traditional" and "new" women: a solution that would only suit one group will be a half-measure. Perhaps cooperating with <span class="tooltip-text" title="Federation of German Women's Associations">**Bund Deutscher Frauenvereine**</span> — an umbrella organization of many disparate feminist organizations — would aid us in finding the way to reconcile the needs of all women in Germany. Unfortunately, as of right now, its rules explicitly bar leftist organizations from joining, but given our efforts we might gain a foothold to urge it to rethink its stance.
  
- @bdf: We stand together with the feminist movement.
- @deny: This isn't our fight.
- @easy_discard: Return card to hand.
- @cancel_advisor_action

